# `resample`

`resample` evaluates a tabulated function on the `x` values supplied by a new
grid file.

## Usage

```text
resample [options] input grid output
```

The input and grid files contain two numeric columns. The grid's second column
is replaced by the interpolated value in the output.

## Options

| Option | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `-h`, `--help` | Print help. |
| `-v` | Print the resolved configuration and verbose diagnostics to standard error. |
| `-vv` | Increase verbosity further. |
| `-V`, `--version` | Print the project version and exit immediately. |
| `-i METHOD`, `--interpolation METHOD` | Select `linear`, `cspline`, `akima`, or `steffen`. Default: `akima`. |
| `-p N` | Write `N` significant digits. Default: `16`. |
| `-e` | Enable constant extrapolation outside the input domain. |
| `-a A` | With `-e`, use `A` below the input domain. Default: the first input `y`. |
| `-b B` | With `-e`, use `B` above the input domain. Default: the last input `y`. |

Without `-e`, no extrapolation is performed and grid points should remain in
the input domain. Both `-a` and `-b` require `-e`.

`linear` is piecewise linear and requires at least two points. `cspline` is a
natural cubic spline and requires at least three points. `akima` is a local
piecewise-cubic interpolant and requires at least five points. `steffen` is a
monotonicity-preserving piecewise-cubic interpolant and requires at least three
points. `cspline` and `akima` can overshoot between samples. `steffen` stays
within each interval's endpoint range and therefore preserves positivity of
nonnegative samples, but its second derivative can be discontinuous and its
shape can be conservative near extrema.

`resample` performs interpolation only. It has no quadrature tolerance,
workspace, rule, or GSL integration error-policy options.
